\section{Discussion and limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What the source models buy.}
The answer is conditional.
When targets resemble the sources, they buy a large variance reduction, about a fifth of the error of stratified sampling, which already uses the sources' item difficulties.
When every target is stronger than every source, predictors fitted on the sources are biased toward the source mean, and what remains over stratified sampling is at most a tenth; a difference correction reduces that bias without removing it, and a weight chosen on an inner split of the sources repairs it only in part: a larger-gap inner split, tried after the main result, helps the logistic predictor and hurts ridge.
The answer depends on the distance above the sources, on the number of source models, and, for ordering, is confined to interpolation and lineage.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Baselines.}
The anchor-point, tinyBenchmarks-style and metabench-style methods are our re-implementations; the anchor clustering is light ($k$-means with one start and $25$ iterations); a stronger clustering, run on the seven leaderboard benchmarks only, changes the ratios by at most $0.10$ and no conclusion; the stronger runs use different draws and have no intervals, so differences of a few percent are within draw noise.
We did not run the assessor, active evaluation acquisition, SubLIME, or the released code of the earlier methods, so nothing here is a statement about those.
\emph{Data.}
The $13$ benchmarks come from two sources; HELM's six share models and the leaderboard's families recur, so they are not $13$ replications, and our intervals reflect the sampling of target families only, not of benchmarks or source models.
Families are derived from model names by keyword (none for LegalBench); cells with few targets are marked thin.
\emph{Selection and tuning.}
Rows named best were chosen on the targets and draws their intervals use; the leave-one-benchmark-out rows are the outcome-independent alternative; the nested split of the sources imitates the distance of the targets above the sources only partly in extrapolation, and the larger-gap split that does better for one predictor was chosen after the main result and is a diagnosis, not a recipe.
\emph{Scope.}
Items are counted, not priced; correctness is binary; no live model was evaluated; and $n=100$ is a different share of the items in different benchmarks.
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/fig3_bias_by_score}
\caption{Signed error (predicted minus true full score, in points) against the target's position in standard deviations of the source models' full scores above (+) or below ($-$) their mean, $n=100$. Each line is the mean error over the non-thin benchmarks with targets in the bin, targets pooled across benchmarks (benchmarks with more targets weigh more); the band is its 95\% family-bootstrap interval. Bins with fewer than five benchmarks or 30 targets are not drawn. Extrapolation targets lie above every source by construction, so that panel has no bins below $+1$.}
\label{fig:bias}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:bias} shows that predictors fitted on the sources compress their estimates toward the source mean: the IRT model, calibrated regression and the anchor plug-in overestimate weak targets and underestimate strong ones, and in extrapolation the IRT model on a uniform design is $5.1$, $6.3$ and $9.7$ points too low in the bins one to two, two to three and above three standard deviations above the sources, and the anchor plug-in $4.9$, $7.4$ and $12.4$.
The sample-mean designs stay within about $0.7$ points of zero, as design-unbiased estimators should; the difference estimator, whose residual term is meant to remove this bias, reduces it to $0.8$, $2.0$ and $3.2$ points (uniform $\times$ difference IRT) but not to zero: the intervals exclude zero.
The variance, computed per target over subset draws, tells the same story (Table~\ref{tab:biasvar}, appendix)
